# Bloomy lesson authoring

- Accuracy first. Base every definition, formula, and worked example on reliable sources such as standard precalculus textbooks or credible references. Do not invent notation or conventions.
- No em dashes anywhere: prose, narration, UI copy, or comments. Use commas, colons, parentheses, or separate sentences.
- Voice: concise, conversational, encouraging, and mathematically precise, never textbook dense. See PRODUCT.md.
- Not every narration beat needs motion. If a beat has nothing to animate, leave `to` and `draw` off, let it read as text, and move on to the next beat.
- Introduce one visual change at a time. Ask the learner to predict or manipulate before revealing an answer. Always offer a hint and a graceful way forward.
- Do not instruct interaction during the watch stage. Save "drag", "slide", "click", or "try" prompts for the try stage (the `practice` line and questions), which appear only once "Your turn" begins. Watch beats narrate and may describe automated motion, but never tell the learner to touch the figure.
- Never break a working lesson. Reuse the shared player and engine and the existing template. Keep the unit circle behavior intact.
- Narration fades in a whole paragraph at a time (not word by word). Keep attention on the text while it reads, and only shift focus to the figure after the beat finishes.
- Render math with KaTeX. Write math in beats and copy using `$...$` (see the bloomy-figures rule).
- Prefer worked examples with concrete numbers and a visible coordinate plane when it makes the idea clearer.
- Over-explain by default. Assume this is the learner's first exposure and actively anticipate exactly where a beginner gets stuck, then address it right there, in the same beat. Define every new term the instant it appears (conjugate, critical point, wall, multiplicity, interval notation, etc.), justify why each step is allowed, and never skip an algebra step. Walk through worked examples one concrete case at a time (for a sign chart, narrate each region and each factor's sign). When unsure whether to add a clarifying sentence, add it. A confused or frustrated learner is a failure, not an edge case: err on the side of too much explanation, never too little.
- Pencil-mimic principle (the main idea). Everything on screen should be something the learner could reproduce with a pencil: number lines, sign charts, labeled points, mirrored roots. The figure is a live version of the hand procedure, not a black box. Avoid effects that cannot be drawn by hand.
- Be explicit about procedure. Name the steps out loud ("the method has three steps: find the critical points, test each interval, keep the sign you want") and use direct "make sure to ___" callouts at the moment they matter (for example, "make sure each test point sits strictly inside an interval"). Use the learner's vocabulary: critical points, zeros, asymptotes/walls.
- Show the work, factor by factor. For any sign analysis, display the sign of each factor per region as a chart (a row per interval, a column per factor, plus the product), and put the current region in focus as the tracer moves. Also annotate the expression itself with the sign above each parenthesized factor.
- Questions must never start already answered. For a manipulate/tracer question, set the starting value OUTSIDE the correct region so the learner has to move to solve it; never let the resting position satisfy the check.
- Every interactive control must visibly move something. A slider/tracer must drive a visible point (and readout) on the figure; if dragging changes nothing on screen, it is a bug.
- Accessibility: support keyboard, pointer, and touch, keep visible focus and readable contrast, and provide reduced-motion fallbacks. Target WCAG 2.2 AA.
